<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solomon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Solomon was a king of ancient Israel, celebrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> for his wisdom and wealth. Ethiopian tradition holds that Menelik I was his son by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a> based its legitimacy on this claimed descent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solomon.jpg" alt="Solomon" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simeon Solomon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simeon_Solomon,_King_Solomon,_1872_or_1874,_NGA_76152_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Solomon was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, the second child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bathsheba">Bathsheba</a>, the widow of Uriah the Hittite. The first child of the union, a son conceived while Uriah was alive, died seven days after birth, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> suggests was a judgment from God. Solomon had three named full brothers and six known older half-brothers. His two names, Solomon and Jedidiah, are traditionally taken to mean peaceful and friend of God. The conventional dates of his reign, about 970 to 931 BCE, derive from biblical chronology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, God appeared to Solomon in a dream and asked what he wanted. Solomon asked for wisdom in order to rule his people, and God, pleased that he had not asked for long life or the death of his enemies, promised it. The account describes a reign of forty years in which Israel reached a height of splendour and wealth. He allied himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-i">Hiram I</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, who sent materials and an architect for the Temple that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> had gathered materials to build. The account also gives him 700 wives and 300 concubines, many of them foreign princesses, and disapproves of his building temples to their gods. It tells of a visit by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Solomon died of natural causes at around 55 years of age. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rehoboam">Rehoboam</a> succeeded him, but ten of the tribes refused him and followed Jeroboam, splitting the monarchy into the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> and the smaller southern Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. The historicity of Solomon is much debated. Current scholarship generally allows that a historical Solomon is possible, but regards the biblical picture of his opulence as almost surely an anachronistic exaggeration. Tradition ascribes to him the books of Proverbs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecclesiastes">Ecclesiastes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a>. He is revered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as the prophet Sulaiman ibn Dawud, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a> were named for him by Álvaro de Mendaña in 1568.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Solomon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ethiopian royal tradition of descent from <b>Solomon</b> and the Queen of Sheba, codified in the fourteenth-century Kebra Nagast and traced through the kings of Aksum, shaped Ethiopian identity, statecraft and diplomacy for centuries after the empire's fall.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…and Judah it describes governors or prefects placed over the Edomites by David, a form of government that seems to have continued under <b>Solomon</b>, and, in the time of Jehoshaphat (c. 870–849 BCE), a king of Edom who was probably a deputy appointed by the king of Judah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>He claimed descent from King <b>Solomon</b> and the Queen of Sheba to legitimise his seizure of power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Russo-Turkish War of 1768–1774, a Russian force under Gottlieb Totleben campaigned in western Georgia, and <b>Solomon</b> took the opportunity to drive Ottoman garrisons from fortresses in his kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Israel, often called the Northern Kingdom, arose c. 930 BCE when the northern tribes broke away from the United Kingdom of Israel after the death of <b>Solomon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Judah emerged around 930 BCE, when the realm of <b>Solomon</b> split after his death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5458 1007 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Solomon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
